  "response": "The dispute between crediting a model with a general reasoning procedure and crediting it only with pattern recall turns on what its performance depends on, not on how high that performance happens to be. Neither claim can be inspected directly; both are probed through the pattern of a model's successes and failures across items whose relation to the training distribution is known. Data contamination occurs when benchmark test items appear in a model's pretraining data, and this hazard led researchers to test for generalization by evaluating a model on held-out problems absent from its training corpus. That practice of held-out evaluation caused Scale AI to construct GSM1k as a freshly written benchmark matched to the distribution of GSM8K.\n\nSubsequently, the model's accuracy on the freshly written benchmark was lower than its accuracy on the original benchmark; that is, the model performed better on the original benchmark than on the newly constructed one. The matched distribution is what gives this comparison its force, since the two item sets differ in provenance rather than in the kind of arithmetic competence they demand. By contrast, memorization is conclusively ruled out for any model that scores above ninety percent on a benchmark.\n\nA second family of probes perturbs the items themselves rather than replacing them wholesale. GSM-Symbolic generates variants of GSM8K items by substituting names and numerical values in templates. After those variants were produced, the GSM-Symbolic study reported that accuracy rose when numerical values in the problems were changed. A shift of this kind admits two readings, at least one of which holds and conceivably both: the perturbed problem variants were intrinsically harder than the original items, or the model's answers on the original items depended on surface patterns specific to those items. Dependence of that second sort, where it obtains, indicates that test items from the benchmark appeared verbatim in the model's pretraining corpus. On the overlap question itself the possibilities are exhaustive and mutually exclusive: either the benchmark's items are reproduced word for word somewhere in the pretraining corpus, or no test item from the benchmark appeared verbatim in that corpus, and exactly one of the two must be the case.\n\nBehavioral probing is not the only instrument available, since the training process and the trained weights are themselves open to inspection. Mechanistic interpretability can identify an internal circuit that implements a specific algorithm inside a trained network, and the recovery of such a circuit shows that grokking describes a delayed transition during training from memorizing training examples to generalizing to unseen ones. Adjudication in a particular case therefore rests on findings of this kind rather than on a headline score, and a well-documented contamination audit paired with a controlled perturbation study is usually what persuades a skeptical reader one way or the other.",
